The `createPropertyListWithDifferentTypes` method can be improved to handle errors more robustly by adding type checking and exception handling.

```java
public class Test {
    public static String createPropertyList() {
        Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();

        try {
            // Create properties with different types
            Property prop1 = new Property().setType(String.class);
            if (!prop1.getType().equals(String.class)) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Type mismatch for property 'prop1'");
            }

            Property prop2 = new Property().setType(Integer.class);
            if (!prop2.getType().equals(Integer.class)) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Type mismatch for property 'prop2'");
            }

            // Add properties to the map
            map.put("prop1", prop1.toString());
            map.put("prop2", prop2.toString());

        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }

        return map.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(createPropertyList());
    }
}

class Property {
    private final String type;

    public Property() {}

    public Property setType(Class<?> type) throws Exception {
        if (!type.isInstance(type)) {
            throw new Exception("Type mismatch for property 'type'");
        }
        this.type = type;
        return this;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "type: " + type.toString();
    }
}
```

This code adds error checking to the `setType` method and catches any `RuntimeExceptions` that may be thrown. If an exception is caught, it prints the error message instead of crashing.

